@@ -0,0 +1,48 @@
+# Deploying Fizzy on one server
+
+This repository includes a [Kamal](https://kamal-deploy.org/) configuration for running Fizzy, its SQLite databases, uploaded files, and background jobs in one container. Kamal Proxy obtains and renews a Let's Encrypt certificate.
+
+## Prerequisites
+
+You need:
+
+- a Linux server reachable over SSH with ports 80 and 443 open;
+- an A/AAAA DNS record pointing your Fizzy hostname to that server; and
+- Docker on the computer from which you deploy. `kamal setup` installs Docker on the server if necessary.
+
+The defaults assume an AMD64 server and root SSH access. Set `KAMAL_ARCH=arm64` for an ARM server, or configure Kamal's [`ssh` options](https://kamal-deploy.org/docs/configuration/ssh/) if the server uses another account.
+
+## Configure
+
+1. In `config/deploy.yml`, replace `fizzy.example.com` with your hostname. You can instead export `FIZZY_HOST` when running Kamal.
+2. Replace the `MAILER_FROM_ADDRESS` and `SMTP_*` examples with your mail provider's settings.
+3. Generate a VAPID key pair for browser push notifications:
+
+   ```sh
+   bin/rails runner 'key = WebPush.generate_key; puts "VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=#{key.public_key}", "VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=#{key.private_key}"'
+   ```
+
+4. Export the secrets used by `.kamal/secrets`, using the two lines printed above for the VAPID values:
+
+   ```sh
+   export SECRET_KEY_BASE="$(bin/rails secret)"
+   export SMTP_PASSWORD="your-smtp-password"
+   export VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY="your-vapid-public-key"
+   export VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY="your-vapid-private-key"
+   ```
+
+   Keep `SECRET_KEY_BASE` and the VAPID keys unchanged between deploys. Store all values in a password manager rather than committing them. You can edit `.kamal/secrets` to fetch them from your password manager automatically.
+
+Then deploy:
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal setup
+```
+
+Fizzy will be available at `https://your-hostname`. Future changes can be deployed with `bin/kamal deploy`.
+
+## Persistence and background jobs
+
+The `fizzy_storage` Docker volume is mounted at `/rails/storage`. It holds all production SQLite databases and local uploads, so replacing the application container during a deploy does not replace its data. Back up this volume regularly.
+
+Solid Queue runs inside the web container through Puma's existing `solid_queue` plugin. Do not add a separate jobs role unless you also disable `SOLID_QUEUE_IN_PUMA` and move to a deployment architecture suitable for multiple containers.
